Umbra: the deinterlace module without yadif (GPL).

modules/video_filter/deinterlace/yadif.h and yadif_template.h carry FFmpeg's
old GPL-2.0-or-later header (Michael Niedermayer, 2006), and algo_yadif.c
includes them, so a libVLC that links this module links GPL code, although the
module itself declares no licence. Every other file of the module (deinterlace.c,
common.c, helpers.c, merge.c, merge_arm*.S, mmx.h, algo_basic.c, algo_x.c,
algo_phosphor.c, algo_ivtc.c and their headers) is LGPL-2.1-or-later.

This patch leaves yadif and yadif2x out of the module so that it is LGPL only:
  * the four yadif files are no longer built (the build script also deletes them);
  * the modes are no longer offered, in the module or in the core's list;
  * a request for "yadif" (from settings or the libvlc API, which still accepts
    the name) selects "x", and "yadif2x" selects "linear", the LGPL algorithms
    closest at the same output frame rate;
  * an unknown mode falls back to "x" (it used to leave the renderer unset).
"auto", the default, has always meant "x" in VLC 3.0, so default playback is
unchanged. The same patch applies to VLC 3.0.21 + VLCKit 3.6.0's patches
(scripts/vlc-patches) and to the VLC 3.0.x commit libvlcjni pins for libVLC
3.6.5 (scripts/vlc-patches-android): the four files are identical in both.

